Why Is Logic-on-Logic 3D Integration Becoming Important?

Logic-on-Logic 3D integration involves stacking multiple logic semiconductor dies vertically to create more compact and highly integrated computing structures.

This approach allows designers to combine different processing functions within a smaller physical footprint. It can also support shorter communication distances between integrated logic layers.

The technology is particularly relevant to high-performance computing and AI processors, where large amounts of data must move efficiently between processing units.

By moving beyond conventional planar integration, 3D architectures provide additional opportunities to improve system density and functionality.

AI and High-Performance Computing Are Driving Market Growth

One of the major factors influencing the Logic-on-Logic 3D Integration Market is the expansion of artificial intelligence computing.

AI accelerators require substantial processing capability and high-speed communication between different functional blocks. Conventional two-dimensional architectures can face challenges involving interconnect density, signal movement and physical integration.

Logic-on-logic stacking provides another approach by allowing multiple computing layers to be integrated into a single semiconductor structure.

High-performance computing and data-center applications are also increasing demand for advanced semiconductor integration technologies.

Face-to-Face and Face-to-Back Stacking Support Different Architectures

Face-to-face stacking involves positioning two semiconductor dies with their active surfaces facing each other. This architecture can support dense interconnections between logic layers.

Face-to-back stacking represents another integration approach in which the orientation of the dies differs. The choice depends on the required electrical connections, physical architecture and manufacturing process.

Monolithic or sequential 3D integration provides an alternative approach in which additional transistor layers are formed sequentially on an existing semiconductor structure.

Each approach involves different manufacturing and design considerations.

